**Singleton.** A class that allows **exactly one instance** and provides a global access point to it. Typical uses: configuration, logger, connection or thread pool. Implementation: make the constructor private, delete copy/assignment, and expose a static accessor.

```cpp
class Logger {
  Logger() = default;
public:
  Logger(const Logger&) = delete;
  Logger& operator=(const Logger&) = delete;
  static Logger& instance() { static Logger inst; return inst; }  // thread-safe since C++11
};
```
Drawbacks: global state that hides dependencies and makes unit testing hard, and tight coupling. Prefer dependency injection when you can.

**Virtual function.** A member function declared `virtual` in a base class, so that calling it through a base pointer or reference runs the **derived** class's override, chosen at **runtime**. This is dynamic dispatch, implemented with a per-class **vtable** and a hidden pointer (vptr) in each object. Without `virtual`, the call is bound at compile time by the static type. Mark overrides with `override`, and make base destructors `virtual` when you delete through a base pointer, otherwise the derived destructor will not run.

**Abstract class.** A class with at least one **pure virtual function** (`virtual void draw() = 0;`). It defines an interface that derived classes must implement.

**Can we instantiate it?** **No.** `Shape s;` is a compile error. You can, however, have pointers or references to it, and create objects of concrete derived classes, such as `Shape* p = new Circle();`. A derived class that does not override all pure virtuals is also abstract.

**Real-world use.** A drawing application with `Shape::draw()` overridden by `Circle` and `Rectangle`, so a single loop renders any shape. Payment methods, plugins and database drivers use the same idea: code to the interface, swap implementations. Java equivalents: abstract classes and interfaces.
